/// The app's logging surface. This is the only file that imports
/// `package:logging`, and the only place `print`/`debugPrint` is still legal —
/// everywhere else logs through one of the named loggers below.
///
/// The ban is on *call sites*, not on the sink's implementation. Leaving this
/// note here saves the next person from "fixing" the one legitimate use.
///
/// ## Level budget
///
/// Levels are a contract, not decoration. Picking one too high is how a log
/// becomes noise nobody reads:
///
/// * [Level.SEVERE]  — a user action failed and did **not** recover; data may
///   be lost. Rare, and must carry the error object and a stack trace.
/// * [Level.WARNING] — degraded but recovered: the operation will retry, a
///   fallback took over, or a peer refused it. Survives into release builds.
/// * [Level.INFO]    — coarse lifecycle, once per *logical* operation. This is
///   the ceiling for "leave it on", so it has to stay quiet enough to read.
/// * [Level.FINE]    — per-operation detail, dev builds only.
/// * [Level.FINER]   — per-row / per-message firehose.
///
/// The rule that keeps this honest: **if a line would print more than once per
/// user action, it belongs at [Level.FINE] or below.** Never log inside
/// `build()`, and never per frame.
///
/// When choosing between WARNING and SEVERE, the question is not how bad it
/// feels — it is whether the operation *recovered*.
///
/// ## Cost
///
/// [Logger.log] checks `isLoggable` before it does anything else, so a call at
/// a disabled level costs one comparison. Three consequences:
///
/// 1. Pass a **closure** for any message that interpolates. Dart evaluates
///    arguments eagerly, so `log.fine('resolved $n')` builds that string even
///    when the level is off; `log.fine(() => 'resolved $n')` does not. Plain
///    strings are fine for `warning`/`severe`, which are rare and usually on.
/// 2. Put work that exists *only* to build a message inside the closure too —
///    a lookup or a count above the log call runs unconditionally.
/// 3. Leave the package's top-level `recordStackTraceAtLevel` at its
///    `Level.OFF` default. Lowering it makes every record at or above that
///    level call `StackTrace.current`, which the package itself flags as
///    expensive. Sites that have a stack pass it explicitly.
///
/// ## Never log
///
/// Credentials; certificate or key material; auth tokens and the headers
/// carrying them (log method, path and status — never the whole header map);
/// request or message payloads (log an endpoint and a byte length instead);
/// user identifiers a human would recognise, such as email or display name;
/// amounts, balances and account numbers in anything financial; and **free text
/// the user typed** — notes, memos, payee or merchant names, custom labels.
///
/// That last one is the entry people miss. No field name marks it as sensitive,
/// it reads as ordinary application data, and it is exactly what gets
/// interpolated during debugging. Treat user-authored strings as personal.
///
/// Passing a caught exception as the `error` argument is fine — the rule is
/// about not reaching into a model for a secret field, not about scrubbing
/// exception text.
///
/// Also check what logs *around* these loggers: an HTTP client's own logging
/// interceptor prints whole bodies and headers and never passes through here,
/// so none of the above applies to it.
library;

import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'package:logging/logging.dart';

// Replace `myapp` with the project name, and the children with this project's
// actual subsystems. Choose them by where you would look during an incident,
// not by folder layout — five or six is plenty. A logger per feature folder
// produces names nobody remembers and no filtering benefit.

/// The root of the app's hierarchy — for the global error handlers in `main()`,
/// which catch things attributable to no single subsystem.
final Logger appLog = Logger('myapp');

/// Backend calls, transport, and retries.
final Logger apiLog = Logger('myapp.api');

/// Local persistence and cache lifecycle.
final Logger storeLog = Logger('myapp.store');

/// Sign-in, session, and token refresh.
final Logger authLog = Logger('myapp.auth');

bool _initialised = false;

/// Sets the root level and attaches the single log sink.
///
/// Call this first in `main()`, ahead of every other bootstrap step. Bootstrap
/// is where destructive recovery lives — cache resets, corrupt-store deletion —
/// and those paths log the only explanation a user will ever get for missing
/// data. A logger installed after them captures nothing.
///
/// Release builds keep [Level.WARNING] and above so a bug report captured from
/// a real device (`adb logcat`, Console.app) still carries something useful;
/// everything below is gated off and costs a comparison.
///
/// Safe to call more than once — a second call would otherwise attach a second
/// listener and duplicate every line, which also makes this safe from tests.
void initLogging() {
  if (_initialised) return;
  _initialised = true;

  Logger.root.level = kReleaseMode ? Level.WARNING : Level.FINE;
  Logger.root.onRecord.listen(_emit);
}

/// Writes a record via [debugPrint], which throttles output so Android's
/// logcat does not drop lines under bursty logging — and which satisfies the
/// `avoid_print` lint that most strict lint sets enable.
///
/// The three fields are formatted separately on purpose. [LogRecord.toString]
/// renders only `[LEVEL] loggerName: message`, so a sink that interpolates the
/// whole record — `debugPrint('$record')` — throws `error` and `stackTrace`
/// away, and every exception passed correctly at a call site vanishes here.
void _emit(LogRecord record) {
  debugPrint('[${record.level.name}] ${record.loggerName}: ${record.message}');

  final error = record.error;
  if (error != null) debugPrint('  error: $error');

  final stackTrace = record.stackTrace;
  if (stackTrace != null) debugPrintStack(stackTrace: stackTrace, maxFrames: 8);
}
